# Code style and review

Nothing below is enforced by tooling: `package.json` has `test` and `typecheck`,
one devDependency, no eslint, no prettier, no CI. Every rule is held up by a human.

## Naming

- TypeScript is `camelCase`, SQL is `snake_case`, and `src/db` is the seam they
  should meet at. Nothing maps `site_id` -> `siteId` today, so raw rows reach
  `domain` behind a cast. Fix that in `src/db`, never by renaming in `domain`.
- A variable holding energy carries its unit: `wh` is Wh, `kwh` is kWh (ADR 001
  makes Wh canonical). A bare `value` passes only beside a `unit` field, as on
  `RawReading`; the bare `total` in `rollup.ts` and `readings.ts` is not a model.
- Timestamp parameters end in `Iso` (`onIso`, `fromIso`); interface fields predate
  the rule (`takenAt`, `bucket`, `validFrom`); new ones take it. All are UTC ISO-8601.
- `Date` objects stay inside `src/lib/dates.ts` - nothing even calls `toUtcIso`.
  `Date.parse` on an ISO string in `domain/tariff.ts` is the one use outside it.
- Error classes are the bare noun (`NotFound`, `Invalid`); only `DomainError` keeps
  the suffix. A new one picks its `code` and status in the same PR; the base is 422.

## Imports

`module`/`moduleResolution` are `NodeNext` and the package is `"type": "module"`,
so a relative import carries `.js` though the file on disk is `.ts`:
`import { toKwh } from "../lib/units.js"`. Without the extension `typecheck` fails;
with no build step it is the only thing catching a bad specifier before boot.

- Order in practice: a whole-module `import type` goes last; the rest is grouped
  loosely by layer, not sorted. Mixed value/type from one module is inline:
  `{ sumWh, type Reading }`, `{ requireApiKey, problem, type Req, type Res }`.
- Do not reorder imports in a PR that is not about imports. With no sorter the
  diff is pure noise and it buries the one line that mattered.
- `tsconfig.json` declares `baseUrl` and `paths: { "@lib/*": ["src/lib/*"] }`. No
  file uses either, nothing rewrites the alias at runtime, and TS 6 deprecates
  `baseUrl`. Delete them rather than adopt them.

## Strictness

| Setting | State | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `strict` | on | `strictNullChecks` is the point: a missing tariff or reading must be a type error, not a `NaN` in pence |
| `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` | off | Wanted, not yet affordable. Its absence is why `active[0]` in `chargeFor` typechecks - see ADR 002 |

`strict` is the only strictness flag; the rest is `target` and the `NodeNext` pair.
`as never` and `as unknown as X` cross `db` -> `domain` today - `api/billing.ts`,
`api/readings.ts`, `workers/rollup.ts`, `db/sitesRepo.ts`, all at the call site.
Each is a review flag: the fix is one typed row mapper in `src/db`, not a cast.

## File size

Soft cap 120 lines, hard 200; the largest file in `src/` is 31 today. Nothing is
near the cap, so a file that reaches it has taken on work that belongs elsewhere.

## Comments

- Comment the rule a signature cannot express, nothing else. The model is
  `Tariff.rate`: "Pence per kWh, except capacity which is pence per kVA per day."
- JSDoc an export only when it carries an invariant (`normalizeUnit`, `toKwh`, the
  `Reading` interface). No `@param`/`@returns`, no commented-out code, no bare TODO.

## Commits and PRs

- `type(scope): subject`, imperative, lowercase; scope is the directory, e.g.
  `fix(domain): apply tariff precedence in chargeFor`.
- A commit that changes money names the ADR in its body. `git log
  src/domain/billing.ts` is the first thing read during a billing incident.
- Under 300 changed lines. Billing arithmetic ships alone, never bundled with a
  rename or an import cleanup. Migrations are additive and go in their own PR.

## Reviewing a change to billing arithmetic

Two approvals, one from someone who has run a settlement. Before approving:

1. Units. For every `number` on the path to `pence`, is it Wh or kWh? ADR 001:
   Wh is canonical, `toKwh` is a presentation-boundary call.
2. Precedence. ADR 002: `capacity` > `timeOfUse` > `fixed`, later `validFrom`
   breaks a tie. `activeTariffs` is unordered; `chargeFor` still takes `active[0]`.
3. Kind. `chargeFor` never reads `Tariff.kind`. A `capacity` rate is pence per kVA
   per day; multiplying it by kWh is wrong by about the site's load factor.
4. Rounding. One `Math.round` to whole pence at the end, never on a partial sum.
5. Test. Assert an exact pence figure computed by hand, not pasted from output.
6. Precision. `readings.wh` is `BIGINT`, TypeScript carries it as `number`; a sum
   that could pass 2^53 Wh has to be called out.

"Looks right" is not an approval on this path. Reproduce the number.
